# Game Design And Development Planning Documentation

Status: active reusable documentation index
Scope: principles, research and a reusable workflow for developing a game idea into a reviewable, evidence-driven Game Planning Draft.

## 1. Purpose

This repository documents how to reason about a game before and during early development without forcing every project into a large document hierarchy.

A concrete game may begin as one note. That note may gradually become a `Game Planning Draft`. New files are created only when separate ownership, review, research, testing, reuse, change tracking or navigation makes them genuinely useful.

```text
one note
  → clearer sections
  → evolving Game Planning Draft
  → linked detail only where it reduces total complexity
```

The reusable documentation exists in advance so a new project does not have to rediscover the basic reasoning model every time.

## 2. Core Planning Model

```text
Audience + Purchase Fantasy
  ↔ Target Player Experience
  ↔ Dominant Reference + Controlled Transformation
  → Mechanics / Rules
  → Dynamics
  → Observable Player Behavior
  → Delivered Player Experience
  → Playtest, prototype and market evidence
  → revision.
```

The model is iterative. Research or evidence may change the audience, promise, reference baseline, mechanics, scope or next development step.

## 3. Start Here

For planning a concrete game, read:

1. [`game-development-planning-workflow.md`](game-development-planning-workflow.md)
2. the principle owners relevant to the current question;
3. the linked research when deeper evidence, examples or source context is needed.

The seven principle-and-terminology owners are not mandatory sequential phases. They are reusable reasoning systems revisited throughout planning.

For observing and analyzing an existing game, use the [`Game Analysis Reference`](game-analysis-reference/README.md):

- [`Player Experience And Anti-Experience`](game-analysis-reference/player-experience-and-anti-experience.md) for experience conditions, behavioral indicators and failure modes;
- [`Observation Flow And Moment Prompts`](game-analysis-reference/observation-flow-and-moment-prompts.md) for optional prompts tied to specific moments of interaction;
- [`Game Structure Analysis`](game-analysis-reference/game-structure-analysis.md) for detailed questions about the game's promise, loops, systems, information, content and production burden.

The reference files ask reusable questions. Principle owners retain definitions and principles; an analysis of one concrete game owns its observations and conclusions.

